</span>
<span id="A4.F15.pic1.p2.pic1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<span id="A4.F15.pic1.p2.pic1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.F15.pic1.p2.pic1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">JensUn:</span> <span id="A4.F15.pic1.p2.pic1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#DCE6FA;">No idea idea idea idea</span></span>
<span id="A4.F15.pic1.p2.pic1.p5.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.F15.pic1.p2.pic1.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">JensUn-#:</span> <span id="A4.F15.pic1.p2.pic1.p5.2.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#DCE6FA;"># # # # # # # #</span></span>
<span id="A4.F15.pic1.p2.pic1.p5.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.F15.pic1.p2.pic1.p5.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">JensUn-“whitespace”:</span><span id="A4.F15.pic1.p2.pic1.p5.3.2" class="ltx_text"></span></span>
</span></span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</span></span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</span></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 15: </span><span id="A4.F15.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Sample outputs on successful forgetting across unlearning methods.</span> For a couple of queries from the forge set of LKF where all unlearning methods successfully forget, we show the respective outputs. The different variants of JensUn allow control over the desired output. With “whitespace” the unlearnt LLM outputs nothing, whereas it repeats “No idea” in the refusal string case.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A5" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix E </span>Extended Discussions</h2>

<section id="A5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.1 </span>Worst-case evaluation</h3>

<div id="A5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Since the ideal goal is to find any information from <math id="A5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D_{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D_{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> is encoded in the model, a sample wise worst-case over the paraphrases would measure the forget quality better than average case. Let <math id="A5.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{i}^{(j)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>I</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{i}^{(j)}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the value of <math id="A5.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{I}(p(x)=y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕀</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{I}(p(x)=y)</annotation></semantics></math> label for the model output matching the GT answer at index sample <math id="A5.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> for it’s <math id="A5.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>-th paraphrase, where <math id="A5.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\{1,2,\dots,N\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\{1,2,\dots,N\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A5.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\in\{1,2,\dots,m\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\in\{1,2,\dots,m\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, the cumulative worst-case accuracy after <math id="A5.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> paraphrases is defined as:</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="A5.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{WorstCaseAvg}^{(k)}=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\mathop{\rm max}\nolimits_{1\leq j\leq k}I_{i}^{(j)}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mtext>WorstCaseAvg</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">max</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>I</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{WorstCaseAvg}^{(k)}=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\mathop{\rm max}\nolimits_{1\leq j\leq k}I_{i}^{(j)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This value is non-decreasing with <math id="A5.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e.,</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="A5.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{WorstCaseAvg}^{(1)}\leq\text{WorstCaseAvg}^{(2)}\leq\dots\leq\text{WorstCaseAvg}^{(m)}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mtext>WorstCaseAvg</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>≤</mo><msup><mtext>WorstCaseAvg</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>≤</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>≤</mo><msup><mtext>WorstCaseAvg</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{WorstCaseAvg}^{(1)}\leq\text{WorstCaseAvg}^{(2)}\leq\dots\leq\text{WorstCaseAvg}^{(m)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Then, the final accuracy (as evaluated by LLM-Judge) on the forget set with <math id="A5.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> samples is <math id="A5.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{WorstCaseAvg}^{(m)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext>WorstCaseAvg</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{WorstCaseAvg}^{(m)}</annotation></semantics></math>. We use <math id="A5.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{J}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{J}</annotation></semantics></math> to denote the worst-case accuracy throughout this work, specifically worst-case over paraphrases is written as <math id="A5.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{J}_{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{J}_{P}</annotation></semantics></math>, worst-case over ICR queries as <math id="A5.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{J}_{ICR}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{J}_{ICR}</annotation></semantics></math> and the worst-case over both these as <math id="A5.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{J}_{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{J}_{W}</annotation></semantics></math>. We illustrate the benefits of our proposed worst-case evaluations using paraphrase questions (PQ) and in-context retain set (ICR) queries in Figure <a href="#A2.F12" title="Figure 12 ‣ B.5 Relearning experiments ‣ Appendix B Experimental Details ‣ Unlearning That Lasts: Utility-Preserving, Robust, and Almost Irreversible Forgetting in LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.2 </span>KL-Divergence for unlearning</h3>

<div id="A5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Similar to the JSD based loss employed by JensUn, one can try loss functions that are lower bounded (we are minimizing the probability of LLM w.r.t a <math id="A5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{\text{target}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mtext>target</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{\text{target}}</annotation></semantics></math>). The simplest alternative to JSD is <math id="A5.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{KL}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{KL}</annotation></semantics></math> (Kullback-Leibler divergence). For the forget set, we take the <math id="A5.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="D_{KL}(P||Q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{KL}(P||Q)</annotation></semantics></math> between the distribution of the current model (<math id="A5.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>) and one-hot distribution of the target token <math id="A5.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{\text{target}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mtext>target</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{\text{target}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Note, in difference to JSD, we do not have the mixture distribution <math id="A5.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A5.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{KL}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{KL}</annotation></semantics></math> is not bounded above. Same as in <a href="#S3.E6" title="In 3.1 Unlearning via JensUn ‣ 3 Unlearning via the Jensen-Shannon Divergence ‣ Unlearning That Lasts: Utility-Preserving, Robust, and Almost Irreversible Forgetting in LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, for the retain term we can again use <math id="A5.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{KL}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{KL}</annotation></semantics></math>, where we minimize between <math id="A5.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A5.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\theta_{\text{ref}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\theta_{\text{ref}}}</annotation></semantics></math> (the distribution of the base model). The gradients of KL-divergence as a loss are bounded, but JSD’s are further bounded by a factor <math id="A5.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math> to that of KL’s, see the proof in <a href="#A5.SS4" title="E.4 Gradient Analysis of JSD and KL-Divergence ‣ Appendix E Extended Discussions ‣ Unlearning That Lasts: Utility-Preserving, Robust, and Almost Irreversible Forgetting in LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In <a href="#A5.T16" title="In E.2 KL-Divergence for unlearning ‣ Appendix E Extended Discussions ‣ Unlearning That Lasts: Utility-Preserving, Robust, and Almost Irreversible Forgetting in LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a>, we show how this <math id="A5.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{KL}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{KL}</annotation></semantics></math> based loss works for unlearning the LKF dataset, we do a small grid-search over the LR and keep the other parameters same as for JensUn. One sees, at lower LR’s <math id="A5.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{KL}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{KL}</annotation></semantics></math>-loss is unable to unlearn the forget set at all. For <span id="A5.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">LR = 5e-6</span>, <math id="A5.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{J}_{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{J}_{W}</annotation></semantics></math> goes down to 1% but the utility of the model is severely degraded. On looking at the training logs, we see that the retain loss grows pretty fast (since this loss is not upper-bounded above) and then never really recovers. Also, all throughout training, the forget loss is magnitudes larger in scale than the retain loss, making LR schedule, and hyperparameter tuning a big factor for <math id="A5.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{KL}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{KL}</annotation></semantics></math> loss. This problem is avoided by JSD by having bounded terms for both the retain and forget terms which take up values on a similar scale, as can be seen in <a href="#A5.F16" title="In E.2 KL-Divergence for unlearning ‣ Appendix E Extended Discussions ‣ Unlearning That Lasts: Utility-Preserving, Robust, and Almost Irreversible Forgetting in LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A5.T16" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 16: </span><span id="A5.T16.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">A <math id="A5.T16.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\text{KL}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">KL</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\text{KL}}</annotation></semantics></math> loss is not effective for unlearning.</span> On unlearning the LKF dataset in the setup from <a href="#S5.T1" title="In 5 Unlearning Experiments ‣ Unlearning That Lasts: Utility-Preserving, Robust, and Almost Irreversible Forgetting in LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, we find the Kullback-Leibler divergence (<math id="A5.T16.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\text{KL}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mtext>KL</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\text{KL}}</annotation></semantics></math>) loss does not yield a good unlearnt yet efficacious LLM.</figcaption>
<table id="A5.T16.4" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="A5.T16.4.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A5.T16.4.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt"></td>
<td id="A5.T16.4.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt"></td>
<td id="A5.T16.4.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_tt">
<span id="A5.T16.4.1.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:34.1pt;">
<span id="A5.T16.4.1.3.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center">For (<math id="A5.T16.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\downarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\downarrow</annotation></semantics></math>)</span>
</span></td>
<td id="A5.T16.4.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt">
<span id="A5.T16.4.1.4.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:31.3pt;">
<span id="A5.T16.4.1.4.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center">Ret (<math id="A5.T16.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\uparrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\uparrow</annotation></semantics></math>)</span>
</span></td>
<td id="A5.T16.4.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_align_top ltx_border_tt" colspan="3">Utility (<math id="A5.T16.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\uparrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\uparrow</annotation></semantics></math>)</td></tr>
<tr id="A5.T16.4.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A5.T16.4.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_r">
<span id="A5.T16.4.2.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:91.0pt;">
<span id="A5.T16.4.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_left">Method</span>
</span></td>
<td id="A5.T16.4.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_r">
<span id="A5.T16.4.2.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:22.8pt;">
<span id="A5.T16.4.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center">LR</span>
</span></td>
<td id="A5.T16.4.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top">
<span id="A5.T16.4.2.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:34.1pt;">
<span id="A5.T16.4.2.3.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.T16.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{J}_{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{J}_{W}</annotation></semantics></math></span>
</span></td>
