<figure id="A5.F16" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.02820v1/loss_curve_jensun.svg" id="A5.F16.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:521/152;" width="521" height="152"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 16: </span><span id="A5.F16.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Training dynamics between accuracy and different losses of JensUn.</span> In this plot for the LKF dataset, we show how forget/retain accuracies and losses look as a function of unlearning steps starting from the pre-trained LLM. Firstly, in terms of forget set, already after 100 unlearning steps the accuracy is 0% and the loss saturates around it’s final value at 200 steps. Although one can stop the unlearning here, the retain set performance at this point is not optimal. The retain set performance degrades from steps 0 to 50, corroborated by the loss going up from an initial value of 0 to <math id="A5.F16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx 0.6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx 0.6</annotation></semantics></math>. On further unlearning, the retain loss goes down and saturate at around step 175 where the retain accuracy reaches the same level as that of the pre-trained LLM. The training curve shows how unlearning for longer helps JensUn attain both better unlearning quality and preserve the original model’s utility, with both losses operating on a very similar scale.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.3 </span>Comparing losses</h3>

<div id="A5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section we analyze the <span id="A5.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">losses</span> of some the methods used in this work: Jensen-Shannon Divergence (JSD) loss (JensUn), the Negative Policy Optimization (NPO) loss, the SimNPO loss, and the modified Negative Log-Likelihood (NLL) loss used by GradAscent and GradDiff. On top of the forget losses as defined below, all these methods (barring GradAscent) also use a retain loss term, which is the standard NLL-loss for NPO, SimNPO, and GradDiff.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SSx1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Properties of loss functions</h3>

<div id="A5.SSx1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="A5.SSx1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> represent the parameters of the model, <math id="A5.SSx1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\theta}(y|x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\theta}(y|x)</annotation></semantics></math> (or <math id="A5.SSx1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}(y|x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}(y|x)</annotation></semantics></math>) denotes the model’s predicted probability distribution over output <math id="A5.SSx1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> given input <math id="A5.SSx1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="A5.SSx1.SSSx1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Jensen-Shannon (JS) Divergence Loss (<math id="A5.SSx1.SSSx1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\text{JensUn }}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>JensUn </mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\text{JensUn }}</annotation></semantics></math>).</h4>

<div id="A5.SSx1.SSSx1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SSx1.SSSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.SSx1.SSSx1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Forget set loss.</span> For the forget set, <math id="A5.SSx1.SSSx1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D_{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D_{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> = <math id="A5.SSx1.SSSx1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y)_{i=1}^{\mathcal{N_{F}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></msub></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y)_{i=1}^{\mathcal{N_{F}}}</annotation></semantics></math> , given the model’s output distribution <math id="A5.SSx1.SSSx1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\theta}(y|x_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\theta}(y|x_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> for a forgotten data point <math id="A5.SSx1.SSSx1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{i},y_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{i},y_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, and denoting <math id="A5.SSx1.SSSx1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{y^{\textrm{target}}_{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi><mtext>target</mtext></msubsup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{y^{\textrm{target}}_{t}}</annotation></semantics></math> the one-hot distribution of the token <math id="A5.SSx1.SSSx1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{\textrm{target}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi><mtext>target</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{\textrm{target}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> over the vocabulary size, the forget loss <math id="A5.SSx1.SSSx1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathcal{F}}^{\text{JSD }}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mtext>JSD </mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathcal{F}}^{\text{JSD }}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as</p>
<table id="A5.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathcal{F}}^{\text{JSD }}(\theta,\mathcal{D_{F}})=\frac{1}{N_{\mathcal{F}}}\sum_{(x,y)\in\mathcal{D_{F}}}\sum_{t=1}^{|y^{\textrm{target}}|}\;\text{JSD }\left(p_{\theta}(y_{t}|x,y^{\textrm{target}}_{&lt;t})\parallel\delta_{y^{\textrm{target}}_{t}}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mtext>JSD </mtext></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>N</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></msub></mfrac><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></msub></mrow></munder><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mtext>target</mtext></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></munderover><mtext>JSD </mtext><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mtext>target</mtext></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi><mtext>target</mtext></msubsup></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathcal{F}}^{\text{JSD }}(\theta,\mathcal{D_{F}})=\frac{1}{N_{\mathcal{F}}}\sum_{(x,y)\in\mathcal{D_{F}}}\sum_{t=1}^{|y^{\textrm{target}}|}\;\text{JSD }\left(p_{\theta}(y_{t}|x,y^{\textrm{target}}_{&lt;t})\parallel\delta_{y^{\textrm{target}}_{t}}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A5.SSx1.SSSx1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SSx1.SSSx1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The Jensen-Shannon (JS) divergence between two probability distributions <math id="A5.SSx1.SSSx1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A5.SSx1.SSSx1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as:</p>
<table id="A5.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\text{JSD}(P\ ||\ Q)=\frac{1}{2}D_{KL}(P\ ||\ M)+\frac{1}{2}D_{KL}(Q\ ||\ M)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>JSD</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo fence="false" lspace="0.500em" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.667em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo fence="false" lspace="0.500em" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.667em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo fence="false" lspace="0.500em" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.667em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{JSD}(P\ ||\ Q)=\frac{1}{2}D_{KL}(P\ ||\ M)+\frac{1}{2}D_{KL}(Q\ ||\ M)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SSx1.SSSx1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A5.SSx1.SSSx1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=\frac{1}{2}(P+Q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=\frac{1}{2}(P+Q)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A5.SSx1.SSSx1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{KL}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{KL}</annotation></semantics></math> is the Kullback-Leibler divergence.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SSx1.SSSx1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SSx1.SSSx1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We minimize <math id="A5.SSx1.SSSx1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathcal{F}}^{\text{JSD }}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mtext>JSD </mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathcal{F}}^{\text{JSD }}</annotation></semantics></math>, which drives <math id="A5.SSx1.SSSx1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\theta}(y|x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\theta}(y|x)</annotation></semantics></math> to become identical to <math id="A5.SSx1.SSSx1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{\textrm{target}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>y</mi><mtext>target</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{\textrm{target}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The Jensen-Shannon divergence is a symmetric and bounded metric. The loss is also fully bounded, <math id="A5.SSx1.SSSx1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq\mathcal{L}_{F}^{\text{JSD }}\leq\log 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>F</mi><mtext>JSD </mtext></msubsup><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq\mathcal{L}_{F}^{\text{JSD }}\leq\log 2</annotation></semantics></math>. The minimum value of <math id="A5.SSx1.SSSx1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> is attained when <math id="A5.SSx1.SSSx1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\theta}(y|x)=y^{\textrm{target}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mtext>target</mtext></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\theta}(y|x)=y^{\textrm{target}}</annotation></semantics></math> for all points in <math id="A5.SSx1.SSSx1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D_{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D_{F}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Through the mixture distribution M, JSD tends to have more stable gradients that remain finite, especially helpful at the start of unlearning where the overlap between distributions can be poor. The model is gently guided towards the desired "forgotten" state without extreme parameter updates. This stability helps prevent the unlearning process from catastrophically damaging the model’s performance on other, non-forgotten data. This can be visualized in <a href="#A5.F16" title="In E.2 KL-Divergence for unlearning ‣ Appendix E Extended Discussions ‣ Unlearning That Lasts: Utility-Preserving, Robust, and Almost Irreversible Forgetting in LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a>, where we can see that at the end of fine-tuning, the forget loss is non-zero and the forget set accuracy is close to zero, indicating the “sweet spot” required has been reached. This also shows that for this particular setup, disjoint distributions are not need (forget loss <math id="A5.SSx1.SSSx1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\neq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≠</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\neq</annotation></semantics></math> 0). A proof regarding the bounded gradients of JSD w.r.t Kl-divergence can be found in <a href="#A5.SS4" title="E.4 Gradient Analysis of JSD and KL-Divergence ‣ Appendix E Extended Discussions ‣ Unlearning That Lasts: Utility-Preserving, Robust, and Almost Irreversible Forgetting in LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SSx1.SSSx1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SSx1.SSSx1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.SSx1.SSSx1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Retain loss.</span>
For the retain set <math id="A5.SSx1.SSSx1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D_{R}}=\{(x,y)_{i}\}_{i=1}^{N_{\mathcal{R}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi></msub></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D_{R}}=\{(x,y)_{i}\}_{i=1}^{N_{\mathcal{R}}}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A5.SSx1.SSSx1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\mathcal{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\mathcal{R}}</annotation></semantics></math> samples, we want the unlearnt model to produce the same output distribution as the base model parameterized by <math id="A5.SSx1.SSSx1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{\textrm{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{\textrm{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus, we minimize the JSD between these two distributions, i.e.</p>
<table id="A5.EGx8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A5.E8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A5.E8.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\mathcal{R}}^{\text{JSD }}(\theta,\mathcal{D_{R}})=\frac{1}{N_{\mathcal{R}}}\sum_{(x,y)\in\mathcal{D_{R}}}\sum_{t=1}^{|y|}\;\text{JSD }\left(p_{\theta}(y_{t}|x,y_{&lt;t})\parallel p_{\theta_{\textrm{ref}}}(y_{t}|x,y_{&lt;t})\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mtext>JSD </mtext></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>N</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi></msub></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi></msub></mrow></munder></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mtext>JSD </mtext><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\mathcal{R}}^{\text{JSD }}(\theta,\mathcal{D_{R}})=\frac{1}{N_{\mathcal{R}}}\sum_{(x,y)\in\mathcal{D_{R}}}\sum_{t=1}^{|y|}\;\text{JSD }\left(p_{\theta}(y_{t}|x,y_{&lt;t})\parallel p_{\theta_{\textrm{ref}}}(y_{t}|x,y_{&lt;t})\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SSx1.SSSx1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">The unlearnt model is initialized at the base model, i.e. <math id="A5.SSx1.SSSx1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta=\theta_{\textrm{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta=\theta_{\textrm{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math>, so at the beginning of fine-tuning <math id="A5.SSx1.SSSx1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathcal{R}}^{\text{JSD }}(\theta,\mathcal{D_{R}})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mtext>JSD </mtext></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathcal{R}}^{\text{JSD }}(\theta,\mathcal{D_{R}})=0</annotation></semantics></math>. The retain loss term does not contribute anything to the overall gradient. As <math id="A5.SSx1.SSSx1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> gets updated to minimize the forget loss, its output distribution will start diverging from the original one, the retain loss enforces that it remains sufficiently close to it, this can be seen in <a href="#A5.F16" title="In E.2 KL-Divergence for unlearning ‣ Appendix E Extended Discussions ‣ Unlearning That Lasts: Utility-Preserving, Robust, and Almost Irreversible Forgetting in LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a>. Overall, the combination of both the bounded loss terms yields a well-behaved yet unlearnt LLM. Combining the two losses defined above, we get JensUn objective</p>
<table id="A5.EGx9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A5.E9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A5.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\text{JensUn }}(\theta,\mathcal{D_{F}},\mathcal{D_{R}})=\mathop{\rm min}\nolimits_{\theta}\biggl(\lambda_{\mathcal{F}}\mathcal{L}_{\mathcal{F}}^{\text{JSD }}(\theta,\mathcal{D_{F}})+\lambda_{\mathcal{R}}\mathcal{L}_{\mathcal{R}}^{\text{JSD }}(\theta,\mathcal{D_{R}})\biggr)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>JensUn </mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">min</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mtext>JSD </mtext></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mtext>JSD </mtext></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\text{JensUn }}(\theta,\mathcal{D_{F}},\mathcal{D_{R}})=\mathop{\rm min}\nolimits_{\theta}\biggl(\lambda_{\mathcal{F}}\mathcal{L}_{\mathcal{F}}^{\text{JSD }}(\theta,\mathcal{D_{F}})+\lambda_{\mathcal{R}}\mathcal{L}_{\mathcal{R}}^{\text{JSD }}(\theta,\mathcal{D_{R}})\biggr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SSx1.SSSx2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Negative Preference Optimization (NPO) Forget Loss (<math id="A5.SSx1.SSSx2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{NPO}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>O</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{NPO}</annotation></semantics></math>).</h4>

<div id="A5.SSx1.SSSx2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SSx1.SSSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This loss was adapted to unlearning from DPO. It encourages a specific relationship between the current model’s output <math id="A5.SSx1.SSSx2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}(y|x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}(y|x)</annotation></semantics></math> and a reference probability <math id="A5.SSx1.SSSx2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\text{ref}}(y|x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\text{ref}}(y|x)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SSx1.SSSx2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="A5.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{NPO}}(\theta,\mathcal{D_{F}})=\mathbb{E}_{(x,y)\in\mathcal{D_{F}}}\left[-\frac{2}{\beta}\log\sigma\left(-\beta\log\left(\frac{\pi_{\theta}(y|x)}{\pi_{\text{ref}}(y|x)}\right)\right)\right]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>NPO</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mi>β</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{NPO}}(\theta,\mathcal{D_{F}})=\mathbb{E}_{(x,y)\in\mathcal{D_{F}}}\left[-\frac{2}{\beta}\log\sigma\left(-\beta\log\left(\frac{\pi_{\theta}(y|x)}{\pi_{\text{ref}}(y|x)}\right)\right)\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A5.SSx1.SSSx2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SSx1.SSSx2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="A5.SSx1.SSSx2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\text{ref}}(y|x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\text{ref}}(y|x)</annotation></semantics></math> is the output of the model prior to unlearning, and <math id="A5.SSx1.SSSx2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> is a hyperparameter controlling the sensitivity of the loss. <math id="A5.SSx1.SSSx2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is the sigmoid function. The loss heavily penalizes situations where <math id="A5.SSx1.SSSx2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}(y|x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}(y|x)</annotation></semantics></math> is significantly <span id="A5.SSx1.SSSx2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">greater</span> than <math id="A5.SSx1.SSSx2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\text{ref}}(y|x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\text{ref}}(y|x)</annotation></semantics></math>. Conversely, if <math id="A5.SSx1.SSSx2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}(y|x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}(y|x)</annotation></semantics></math> is much <span id="A5.SSx1.SSSx2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">smaller</span> than <math id="A5.SSx1.SSSx2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\text{ref}}(y|x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\text{ref}}(y|x)</annotation></semantics></math>, the loss approaches <math id="A5.SSx1.SSSx2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>. This encourages the model to reduce its confidence for specific outputs <math id="A5.SSx1.SSSx2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> compared to a reference, effectively "forgetting" or de-emphasizing them. The value inside the outermost <math id="A5.SSx1.SSSx2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log\sigma(\dots)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log\sigma(\dots)</annotation></semantics></math> term, let <math id="A5.SSx1.SSSx2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z=-\beta\log\left(\frac{\pi_{\theta}(y|x)}{\pi_{\text{ref}}(y|x)}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z=-\beta\log\left(\frac{\pi_{\theta}(y|x)}{\pi_{\text{ref}}(y|x)}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>, can range from <math id="A5.SSx1.SSSx2.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\infty</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="A5.SSx1.SSSx2.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+\infty</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
<ul id="A5.I1" class="ltx_itemize" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:2mm;">
<li id="A5.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A5.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As <math id="A5.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z\to+\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z\to+\infty</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e., <math id="A5.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}(y|x)\ll\pi_{\text{ref}}(y|x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≪</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}(y|x)\ll\pi_{\text{ref}}(y|x)</annotation></semantics></math>), <math id="A5.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(z)\to 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(z)\to 1</annotation></semantics></math>, so <math id="A5.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log\sigma(z)\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log\sigma(z)\to 0</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, <math id="A5.I1.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{NPO}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>O</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{NPO}</annotation></semantics></math> approaches <math id="A5.I1.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A5.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A5.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As <math id="A5.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z\to-\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z\to-\infty</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e., <math id="A5.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}(y|x)\gg\pi_{\text{ref}}(y|x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}(y|x)\gg\pi_{\text{ref}}(y|x)</annotation></semantics></math>), <math id="A5.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(z)\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(z)\to 0</annotation></semantics></math>, so <math id="A5.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log\sigma(z)\to-\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log\sigma(z)\to-\infty</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, <math id="A5.I1.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{NPO}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>O</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{NPO}</annotation></semantics></math> approaches <math id="A5.I1.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+\infty</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="A5.SSx1.SSSx2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Therefore, <math id="A5.SSx1.SSSx2.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{NPO}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>O</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{NPO}</annotation></semantics></math> is bounded below by <math id="A5.SSx1.SSSx2.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> but unbounded above (can reach <math id="A5.SSx1.SSSx2.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+\infty</annotation></semantics></math>). As we are minimizing this objective, the lower bound should in principle help to prevent complete destruction of the model. This phenomena holds, from our experiments. But, if <math id="A5.SSx1.SSSx2.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}(y|x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}(y|x)</annotation></semantics></math> is significantly larger than <math id="A5.SSx1.SSSx2.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\text{ref}}(y|x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\text{ref}}(y|x)</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e., the model is not forgetting effectively), the loss can become extremely large. Hence, one needs meticulous hyper-parameter tuning to make <math id="A5.SSx1.SSSx2.p3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{NPO}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>O</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{NPO}</annotation></semantics></math> work effectively for unlearning, as can be seen from its variable performance across datasets (Tables <a href="#S5.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 5 Unlearning Experiments ‣ Unlearning That Lasts: Utility-Preserving, Robust, and Almost Irreversible Forgetting in LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and <a href="#S5.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 5.1 Unlearning the LKF dataset ‣ 5 Unlearning Experiments ‣ Unlearning That Lasts: Utility-Preserving, Robust, and Almost Irreversible Forgetting in LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SSx1.SSSx3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">SimNPO Forget Loss (<math id="A5.SSx1.SSSx3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{SimNPO}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>O</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{SimNPO}</annotation></semantics></math>).</h4>

<div id="A5.SSx1.SSSx3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SSx1.SSSx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In SimNPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fan et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, the authors try to mitigate the reference model bias in NPO by replacing its reward formulation. Specifically, SimNPO removes the NPO losses dependence on <math id="A5.SSx1.SSSx3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{ref}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{ref}</annotation></semantics></math> and instead takes a reference-free but length-normalized reward formulation.</p>
<table id="A5.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{SimNPO}}(\theta,\mathcal{D_{F}})=\mathbb{E}_{(x,y)\in\mathcal{D_{F}}}\left[-\frac{2}{\beta}\log\sigma\left(-\frac{\beta}{|y|}\log(\pi_{\theta}(y|x)-\gamma)\right)\right]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>SimNPO</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mi>β</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>β</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{SimNPO}}(\theta,\mathcal{D_{F}})=\mathbb{E}_{(x,y)\in\mathcal{D_{F}}}\left[-\frac{2}{\beta}\log\sigma\left(-\frac{\beta}{|y|}\log(\pi_{\theta}(y|x)-\gamma)\right)\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SSx1.SSSx3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><math id="A5.SSx1.SSSx3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> is a reward parameter that defines the margin of preference for a desired
response over a non-preferred one, but in practice is often set to 0. <math id="A5.SSx1.SSSx3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> controls the models methods utility and a higher value yields a strong un-learner with reduced utility. Similar to the NPO loss, <math id="A5.SSx1.SSSx3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{SimNPO}}(\theta,\mathcal{D_{F}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>SimNPO</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{SimNPO}}(\theta,\mathcal{D_{F}})</annotation></semantics></math> is also bounded below by 0, but there is no term to control the deviation from the base model. Hence, we find that unlearning with SimNPO often veers away from the reference model and hence it’s utility is starkly degraded in comparison to the base LLM, even when using a retain loss term, see <a href="#S5.T1" title="In 5 Unlearning Experiments ‣ Unlearning That Lasts: Utility-Preserving, Robust, and Almost Irreversible Forgetting in LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SSx1.SSSx4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Log-Likelihood Loss for Unlearning GradAscent and GradDiff </h4>

<div id="A5.SSx1.SSSx4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SSx1.SSSx4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The standard negative log-likelihood (NLL) loss is typically minimized to train a model. For unlearning, the objective is reversed: we want to maximize the NLL for the forgotten data points, which means we want to decrease the probability the model assigns to the true label <math id="A5.SSx1.SSSx4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> for input <math id="A5.SSx1.SSSx4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>. This is achieved by minimizing the log-likelihood loss.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SSx1.SSSx4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="A5.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}(\theta,\mathcal{D_{F}})=\frac{1}{N_{\mathcal{F}}}\sum_{(x,y)\in\mathcal{D}_{\mathcal{F}}}\sum_{t=1}^{|y|}\log p_{\theta}\big(y_{t}^{*}\mid x,y_{&lt;t}\big)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>N</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></msub></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></munderover><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}(\theta,\mathcal{D_{F}})=\frac{1}{N_{\mathcal{F}}}\sum_{(x,y)\in\mathcal{D}_{\mathcal{F}}}\sum_{t=1}^{|y|}\log p_{\theta}\big(y_{t}^{*}\mid x,y_{&lt;t}\big).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SSx1.SSSx4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A5.SSx1.SSSx4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{t}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{t}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is the ground truth for <math id="A5.SSx1.SSSx4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, as one minimizes the loss, this drives <math id="A5.SSx1.SSSx4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\theta}(y|x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\theta}(y|x)</annotation></semantics></math> towards <math id="A5.SSx1.SSSx4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>. Since probabilities <math id="A5.SSx1.SSSx4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\theta}(y|x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\theta}(y|x)</annotation></semantics></math> are between <math id="A5.SSx1.SSSx4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A5.SSx1.SSSx4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A5.SSx1.SSSx4.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> is bounded above by <math id="A5.SSx1.SSSx4.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> but unbounded below (can go to <math id="A5.SSx1.SSSx4.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\infty</annotation></semantics></math>). This occurs when the model’s predicted probability for the true class approaches 0. This unbounded-ness below means the objective provides no incentive to preserve anything from the original model, yielding gibberish content after unlearning. This is true even though one has a retain-set term that encourages legible output, see examples in <a href="#A5.F21" title="In E.4 Gradient Analysis of JSD and KL-Divergence ‣ Appendix E Extended Discussions ‣ Unlearning That Lasts: Utility-Preserving, Robust, and Almost Irreversible Forgetting in LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">21</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SSx1.SSSx5" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Retain losses</h4>

<div id="A5.SSx1.SSSx5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SSx1.SSSx5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For all of NPO, SimNPO, and GradDiff, the NLL loss (cross-entropy) w.r.t the ground truth label is used for preserving the performance on the retain set. Formally, for a batch of size <math id="A5.SSx1.SSSx5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> from the retain set <math id="A5.SSx1.SSSx5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D_{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D_{R}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SSx1.SSSx5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="A5.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{NLL}(\theta,\mathcal{D_{R}})=\frac{1}{N_{\mathcal{F}}}\sum_{(x,y)\in\mathcal{D}_{\mathcal{R}}}\sum_{t=1}^{|y|}-\log p_{\theta}\big(y_{t}^{*}\mid x,y_{&lt;t}\big)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>N</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></msub></mfrac><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi></msub></mrow></munder><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></munderover><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{NLL}(\theta,\mathcal{D_{R}})=\frac{1}{N_{\mathcal{F}}}\sum_{(x,y)\in\mathcal{D}_{\mathcal{R}}}\sum_{t=1}^{|y|}-\log p_{\theta}\big(y_{t}^{*}\mid x,y_{&lt;t}\big).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SSx1.SSSx5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A5.SSx1.SSSx5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math> is the vocabulary set. As one minimizes <math id="A5.SSx1.SSSx5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{NLL}(\theta,\mathcal{D_{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{NLL}(\theta,\mathcal{D_{R}})</annotation></semantics></math>, this drives <math id="A5.SSx1.SSSx5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\theta}(y|x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\theta}(y|x)</annotation></semantics></math> towards <math id="A5.SSx1.SSSx5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(y)</annotation></semantics></math> for the specific input. This is the standard loss used for training LLMs. We note that this term is bounded below by 0.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A5.SSx2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Conclusion</h3>

<div id="A5.SSx2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The <span id="A5.SSx2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Jensen-Shannon divergence loss (<math id="A5.SSx2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{JensUn }}(\theta,\mathcal{D_{F}},\mathcal{D_{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">JensUn </mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{JensUn }}(\theta,\mathcal{D_{F}},\mathcal{D_{R}})</annotation></semantics></math>)</span> stands out as the most robust and stable choice for unlearning. Its inherent boundedness ensures that the loss values remain finite and well-controlled throughout the optimization process. This property helps in keeping both forget and retain losses in a similar range, which is a major concern with unbounded losses like in NPO, SimNPO. While <math id="A5.SSx2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{NPO}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>O</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{NPO}</annotation></semantics></math> offers a powerful mechanism for constraining probabilities and guiding forgetting, its unbounded upper range means careful hyperparameter tuning is needed to manage initial updates. <math id="A5.SSx2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{GD}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{GD}</annotation></semantics></math> is generally unsuitable for direct unlearning due to its potential for large absolute loss values which can catastrophically degrade model performance. Furthermore, the bounded-ness of the gradients (<a href="#A5.SS4" title="E.4 Gradient Analysis of JSD and KL-Divergence ‣ Appendix E Extended Discussions ‣ Unlearning That Lasts: Utility-Preserving, Robust, and Almost Irreversible Forgetting in LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.4</span></a>) of <math id="A5.SSx2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{JensUn }}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>JensUn </mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{JensUn }}</annotation></semantics></math> enables longer and smoother training. Therefore, JensUn provides a more predictable and safer approach to integrating unlearning objectives into model training.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.4 </span>Gradient Analysis of JSD and KL-Divergence</h3>

<div id="A5.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we show that the gradient of the JS divergence with respect to the pre-softmax logits is upper-bounded by a scaled version of the gradient of the Kullback-Leibler (KL) divergence.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="A5.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=\textrm{softmax}(u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>softmax</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=\textrm{softmax}(u)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then the gradients of the KL and JS divergences with respect to a logit <math id="A5.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are given by:</p>
<table id="A5.EGx10" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A5.Ex9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A5.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{\partial\textrm{KL}}{\partial u_{i}}(p||q)=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mtext>KL</mtext></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{\partial\textrm{KL}}{\partial u_{i}}(p||q)=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A5.Ex9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle q_{i}-p_{i}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle q_{i}-p_{i},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A5.Ex10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A5.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{\partial\textrm{JS}}{\partial u_{i}}(p||q)=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mtext>JS</mtext></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{\partial\textrm{JS}}{\partial u_{i}}(p||q)=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A5.Ex10.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle q_{i}\left[\frac{1}{2}\log\left(\frac{q_{i}}{m_{i}}\right)-\frac{1}{2}\textrm{KL}(q||m)\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mi>log</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mtext>KL</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle q_{i}\left[\frac{1}{2}\log\left(\frac{q_{i}}{m_{i}}\right)-\frac{1}{2}\textrm{KL}(q||m)\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS4.p2.2" class="ltx_p">with
<math id="A5.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{i}=\frac{p_{i}+q_{i}}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{i}=\frac{p_{i}+q_{i}}{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We analyze the case where the target distribution is a one-hot encoded label: <math id="A5.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=e_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=e_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="A5.SS4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\neq k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\neq k</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="A5.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left|\frac{\partial\textrm{KL}}{\partial u_{i}}\right|=q_{i}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mtext>KL</mtext></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left|\frac{\partial\textrm{KL}}{\partial u_{i}}\right|=q_{i},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS4.p4.2" class="ltx_p">and</p>
<table id="A5.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\left|\frac{\partial\textrm{JS}}{\partial u_{i}}\right|=q_{i}\left|\frac{1}{2}\log\left(\frac{2q_{i}}{q_{i}}\right)-\frac{1}{2}\textrm{KL}(q||m)\right|\leq q_{i}\frac{\log 2}{2}\approx 0.3466\cdot q_{i}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em">|</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mtext>JS</mtext></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo fence="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em">=</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em">|</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mi>log</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mtext>KL</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em">≤</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mfrac><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.3466</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left|\frac{\partial\textrm{JS}}{\partial u_{i}}\right|=q_{i}\left|\frac{1}{2}\log\left(\frac{2q_{i}}{q_{i}}\right)-\frac{1}{2}\textrm{KL}(q||m)\right|\leq q_{i}\frac{\log 2}{2}\approx 0.3466\cdot q_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS4.p4.3" class="ltx_p">since <math id="A5.SS4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{i}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{i}=0</annotation></semantics></math> and</p>
<table id="A5.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="0\leq\frac{1}{2}\textrm{KL}(q||m)\leq\frac{1}{2}\textrm{KL}(q||m)+\frac{1}{2}\textrm{KL}(p||m)=\textrm{JS}(p||q)\leq\log 2." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mtext>KL</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mtext>KL</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mtext>KL</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mtext>JS</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq\frac{1}{2}\textrm{KL}(q||m)\leq\frac{1}{2}\textrm{KL}(q||m)+\frac{1}{2}\textrm{KL}(p||m)=\textrm{JS}(p||q)\leq\log 2.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS4.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="A5.SS4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=k</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="A5.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left|\frac{\partial\textrm{KL}}{\partial u_{i}}\right|=1-q_{i}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mtext>KL</mtext></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left|\frac{\partial\textrm{KL}}{\partial u_{i}}\right|=1-q_{i},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS4.p5.2" class="ltx_p">and</p>
<table id="A5.EGx11" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A5.Ex15"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A5.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\left|\frac{\partial\textrm{JS}}{\partial u_{k}}\right|=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mtext>JS</mtext></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\left|\frac{\partial\textrm{JS}}{\partial u_{k}}\right|=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A5.Ex15.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{q_{k}}{2}\left|\log\left(\frac{2q_{k}}{1+q_{k}}\right)-\textrm{KL}(q||m)\right|=\frac{q_{k}}{2}\left|\log\left(\frac{2q_{k}}{1+q_{k}}\right)-q_{k}\log\left(\frac{2q_{k}}{1+q_{k}}\right)-\sum_{j\neq k}q_{j}\log\left(\frac{2q_{j}}{q_{j}}\right)\right|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em">|</mo><mi>log</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mtext>KL</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em">=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em">|</mo><mi>log</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></munder></mstyle><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{q_{k}}{2}\left|\log\left(\frac{2q_{k}}{1+q_{k}}\right)-\textrm{KL}(q||m)\right|=\frac{q_{k}}{2}\left|\log\left(\frac{2q_{k}}{1+q_{k}}\right)-q_{k}\log\left(\frac{2q_{k}}{1+q_{k}}\right)-\sum_{j\neq k}q_{j}\log\left(\frac{2q_{j}}{q_{j}}\right)\right|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A5.Ex16"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A5.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A5.Ex16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{q_{k}}{2}\left|(1-q_{k})\log\left(\frac{2q_{k}}{1+q_{k}}\right)-\sum_{j\neq k}q_{j}\log 2\right|=\frac{q_{k}}{2}\left|(1-q_{k})\log\left(\frac{2q_{k}}{1+q_{k}}\right)-(1-q_{k})\log 2\right|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{q_{k}}{2}\left|(1-q_{k})\log\left(\frac{2q_{k}}{1+q_{k}}\right)-\sum_{j\neq k}q_{j}\log 2\right|=\frac{q_{k}}{2}\left|(1-q_{k})\log\left(\frac{2q_{k}}{1+q_{k}}\right)-(1-q_{k})\log 2\right|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A5.Ex17"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A5.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A5.Ex17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{q_{k}}{2}(1-q_{k})\left|\log\left(\frac{q_{k}}{1+q_{k}}\right)\right|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{q_{k}}{2}(1-q_{k})\left|\log\left(\frac{q_{k}}{1+q_{k}}\right)\right|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS4.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS4.p6.1" class="ltx_p">We can now show that the term <math id="A5.SS4.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{x}{2}\left|\log\left(\frac{x}{1+x}\right)\right|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{x}{2}\left|\log\left(\frac{x}{1+x}\right)\right|</annotation></semantics></math> is bounded by <math id="A5.SS4.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\log 2}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\log 2}{2}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A5.SS4.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS4.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS4.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Defining <math id="A5.SS4.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x)=\frac{x}{2}\log\frac{x}{1+x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x)=\frac{x}{2}\log\frac{x}{1+x}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="A5.SS4.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lim_{x\rightarrow 0}f(x)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>lim</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lim_{x\rightarrow 0}f(x)=0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A5.SS4.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(1)=-\frac{\log 2}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(1)=-\frac{\log 2}{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A5.SS4.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{\prime}(x)=\frac{1}{2}\left(\log\frac{x}{1+x}-\frac{x}{1+x}+1\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\prime}(x)=\frac{1}{2}\left(\log\frac{x}{1+x}-\frac{x}{1+x}+1\right)</annotation></semantics></math>.
With <math id="A5.SS4.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=\frac{x}{1+x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=\frac{x}{1+x}</annotation></semantics></math>, we get <math id="A5.SS4.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\in[0,\frac{1}{2}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in[0,\frac{1}{2}]</annotation></semantics></math>, and define <math id="A5.SS4.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(y)=2f^{\prime}(y)=\log y-y+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(y)=2f^{\prime}(y)=\log y-y+1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since <math id="A5.SS4.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{\prime}(y)=\frac{1}{y}-1&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>y</mi></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{\prime}(y)=\frac{1}{y}-1&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A5.SS4.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\in[0,\frac{1}{2}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in[0,\frac{1}{2}]</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A5.SS4.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(y)</annotation></semantics></math> is increasing in <math id="A5.SS4.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\in[0,\frac{1}{2}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in[0,\frac{1}{2}]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A5.SS4.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{\prime}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\prime}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="A5.SS4.p7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e. its maximum is attained at <math id="A5.SS4.p7.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since <math id="A5.SS4.p7.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lim_{x\rightarrow 0}f^{\prime}(x)=-\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>lim</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lim_{x\rightarrow 0}f^{\prime}(x)=-\infty</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A5.SS4.p7.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{\prime}(1)=\frac{1}{2}\left(\log\frac{1}{2}+\frac{1}{2}\right)&lt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\prime}(1)=\frac{1}{2}\left(\log\frac{1}{2}+\frac{1}{2}\right)&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A5.SS4.p7.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{\prime}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\prime}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is negative on <math id="A5.SS4.p7.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus, <math id="A5.SS4.p7.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is motonically decreasing in <math id="A5.SS4.p7.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, and since <math id="A5.SS4.p7.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lim_{x\rightarrow 0}f(x)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>lim</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lim_{x\rightarrow 0}f(x)=0</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="A5.SS4.p7.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathop{\rm min}\nolimits_{x\in(0,1]}f(x)=\mathop{\rm max}\nolimits_{x\in(0,1]}|f(x)|=|f(1)|=\frac{\log 2}{2}&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>min</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">max</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathop{\rm min}\nolimits_{x\in(0,1]}f(x)=\mathop{\rm max}\nolimits_{x\in(0,1]}|f(x)|=|f(1)|=\frac{\log 2}{2}&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS4.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS4.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Using this, we get</p>
<table id="A5.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left|\frac{\partial\textrm{JS}}{\partial u_{k}}\right|=\frac{q_{k}}{2}(1-q_{k})\left|\log\left(\frac{q_{k}}{1+q_{k}}\right)\right|\leq(1-q_{k})\frac{\log 2}{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mtext>JS</mtext></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left|\frac{\partial\textrm{JS}}{\partial u_{k}}\right|=\frac{q_{k}}{2}(1-q_{k})\left|\log\left(\frac{q_{k}}{1+q_{k}}\right)\right|\leq(1-q_{k})\frac{\log 2}{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS4.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS4.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, this means</p>
<table id="A5.Ex19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left|\frac{\partial\textrm{JS}}{\partial u_{i}}\right|\leq\left|\frac{\partial\textrm{KL}}{\partial u_{i}}\right|\frac{\log 2}{2}\quad i=1,\ldots,d,\quad\textrm{and}\quad\left\|\nabla_{u}\textrm{JS}\right\|\leq\frac{\log 2}{2}\left\|\nabla_{u}\textrm{KL}\right\|." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mtext>JS</mtext></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mtext>KL</mtext></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mtext>and</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>u</mi></msub><mtext>JS</mtext></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>u</mi></msub><mtext>KL</mtext></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left|\frac{\partial\textrm{JS}}{\partial u_{i}}\right|\leq\left|\frac{\partial\textrm{KL}}{\partial u_{i}}\right|\frac{\log 2}{2}\quad i=1,\ldots,d,\quad\textrm{and}\quad\left\|\nabla_{u}\textrm{JS}\right\|\leq\frac{\log 2}{2}\left\|\nabla_{u}\textrm{KL}\right\|.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
